{"kind":"assert","text":"I have been rediscovering my own open requests instead of reading the queue, and today produced three instances.\nf296743a, filed 2026-08-10, asks to retire the comma-joined artifact paths and re-anchor the documentation resting on them, citing AGENTS.md naming the defect. I filed ee27e6b0 today asking for the same thing in the same terms, and presented it as a new finding. Retired the older as duplicated, naming the survivor.\nbe558440, filed 2026-08-10, asks to stop the merge step manufacturing staleness on every completed loop. I reported that today as assert 2d5d56f3 as though it were newly observed. Worse, be558440 itself records that it replaces f3ccfc1a, retired unimplemented after measurement — so today's report is the third generation of one finding.\n7827ebac, filed 2026-08-09, records two wall-clock assertions failing under machine load, at load averages 31 and 25, and says they would fail on a hosted runner. That is the same class as the finding I made today reviewing head 515cf306, where cmd/gs measured 27.63s against a 30-second timeout with 1.69x observed variance. Codex's proposal 31ffb2ba answers it and is waiting on hugh.\nWhy it keeps happening: eighteen requests I addressed to myself sit unpromised and stale, dating from 2026-08-07 to 2026-08-10, and my tick poll never showed them. It filtered requests addressed to me by commitment status and enumerated the statuses I expected, so stale rows — which are live work — were excluded. The queue was accurate every time; the query asked the wrong question.\nNot retiring the other seventeen. Two of them, be558440 and 7827ebac, are live findings with fresh evidence attached today, and the rest need the same one-at-a-time verification the earlier backlog got, which is recorded in dc32f3d3 and 02bcab10. Recording the pattern now so the next tick starts from the queue rather than from a blank page."}